(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-11"] = {"4.1":{"s":"A definite integral of a continuous function over [a, b] can be defined as the limit of a sum: split the interval into n equal strips, add f at each strip's point times its width, and let n grow without limit. For a positive function this is the area under the curve between x = a and x = b.","f":[{"x":"∫ₐᵇ f dx = lim(n→∞) h ∑ f(a + rh), h = (b − a)/n","n":"Definite integral as a limit of sum"}],"w":["With h = (b − a)/n, the sum uses f(a + rh) for r = 1 to n; let n → ∞ (h → 0) only at the end."],"x":["A definite integral has two limits, a and b, and its value is a number. For a positive function, it is the area between the curve, the x-axis and the lines x = a and x = b. We can define it as a limit of a sum.","Cut [a, b] into n equal strips. Each has width h = (b − a)/n. On the r-th strip take the height f(a + rh). The area of the strip is about h × f(a + rh). Add all the strips and let n grow without limit: the integral equals the limit of h × [f(a + h) + f(a + 2h) + … + f(a + nh)].","To evaluate such a limit you need three sum formulas: 1 + 2 + … + n = n(n + 1)/2; 1² + 2² + … + n² = n(n + 1)(2n + 1)/6; 1³ + 2³ + … + n³ = [n(n + 1)/2]². Put the sum in closed form and then let n tend to infinity.","The same idea works backwards. A limit such as the limit of (1/n)[f(1/n) + f(2/n) + … + f(n/n)] is the integral of f(x) from 0 to 1. Spot h = 1/n and the point r/n, and write the integral.","Common mistake: forgetting the factor h, or letting n tend to infinity too early."],"e":"Find the integral of x from 0 to 2 as a limit. Here h = 2/n and f(rh) = 2r/n. The sum is (2/n) × (2/n)(1 + 2 + … + n) = (4/n²) × n(n + 1)/2 = 2(n + 1)/n. As n tends to infinity this becomes 2."},"4.2":{"s":"Fundamental theorem: if g is any antiderivative of a continuous f, then the integral from a to b equals g(b) − g(a). The constant c cancels, so a definite integral is a number, not a function. a is the lower limit and b the upper limit.","f":[{"x":"∫_a^b f(x) dx = g(b) - g(a)","n":"Where g is an antiderivative of f (g' = f).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.157"}],"w":["No + c in a definite integral; it cancels."],"x":["The fundamental theorem of integral calculus links the two ideas of the course. If g is any antiderivative of a continuous function f, then the integral of f from a to b equals g(b) − g(a). Write it as [g(x)] with limits a and b, and subtract the value at the lower limit from the value at the upper limit.","Method: (1) find an antiderivative using the methods of the last chapter (substitution, parts, partial fractions); (2) put in the upper limit; (3) put in the lower limit; (4) subtract. There is no + c, because it cancels in the subtraction.","When you use substitution, you can change the limits along with the variable. If t = h(x), the new limits are h(a) and h(b), and then you never need to go back to x. If you do go back to x, keep the old limits. Do not mix the two.","With integration by parts, apply the limits to the product term as well, not only to the remaining integral. Check that the function is continuous on [a, b]: the integral of 1/x² from −1 to 1 cannot be found this way, because the function is not defined at 0.","A value can be negative, if the curve is below the axis. Area is then the positive size of that part."],"e":"The integral of 2x/(x² + 1) from 0 to 1. Put t = x² + 1, so dt = 2x dx. New limits: t = 1 and t = 2. The integral is the integral of dt/t from 1 to 2, which is log 2 − log 1 = log 2."},"4.3":{"s":"Properties make definite integrals easier. Swapping the limits changes the sign. The variable's name does not matter. The interval can be split at any point. Replacing x by a + b − x leaves the integral unchanged, which often lets two forms be added to cancel a hard part. Over a symmetric interval [−a, a], an odd function integrates to zero and an even one to twice the integral over [0, a].","f":[{"x":"∫_a^b f(x) dx = -∫_b^a f(x) dx","n":"Swapping the limits changes the sign.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.157"},{"x":"∫_a^b f(x) dx = ∫_a^c f(x) dx + ∫_c^b f(x) dx","n":"Splitting at c, a < c < b.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.158"},{"x":"∫_a^b f(x) dx = ∫_a^b f(a + b - x) dx","n":"King's property (Property V); for limits 0 to a it becomes the integral of f(x) = the integral of f(a - x) (Property VI, p.160).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.159"},{"x":"∫_0^(2a) f(x) dx = ∫_0^a f(x) dx + ∫_0^a f(2a - x) dx","n":"Property VII.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.161"},{"x":"∫_(-a)^a f(x) dx = 2*∫_0^a f(x) dx","n":"If f is even (f(-x) = f(x)); the integral is 0 if f is odd.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.161"}],"w":["Check odd/even first: an odd integrand over [−a, a] gives 0 straight away.","For f(x)/(f(x) + f(a + b − x)) type integrals, add I to its reflected form: 2I = ∫1 dx."],"x":["Basic properties. Swapping the limits changes the sign. The letter used for the variable does not matter. The interval can be split at any point c: the integral from a to b is the integral from a to c plus the integral from c to b. This is how |x| and other piecewise functions are handled: split where the formula changes.","Reflection property. The integral of f(x) from a to b equals the integral of f(a + b − x) from a to b. For limits 0 and a, it reads: the integral of f(x) equals the integral of f(a − x). Method: call the integral I, write it a second time with the reflected variable, add the two, and hope the sum is simple. For f(x)/(f(x) + f(a + b − x)) the sum is the integral of 1, so I = (b − a)/2.","Property for 0 to 2a. The integral of f(x) from 0 to 2a equals the integral from 0 to a of f(x) + f(2a − x). So it is twice the integral from 0 to a if f(2a − x) = f(x), and it is 0 if f(2a − x) = −f(x).","Even and odd functions over [−a, a]. If f(−x) = f(x) (even), the integral is twice the integral from 0 to a. If f(−x) = −f(x) (odd), the integral is 0. Always test for odd or even first: it can save a long calculation.","Common mistakes: using the even rule on a function that is neither odd nor even, and forgetting that the reflection trick needs the right sum a + b."],"e":"Find the integral of sin x/(sin x + cos x) from 0 to π/2. Call it I. Replace x by π/2 − x to get the integral of cos x/(cos x + sin x), which is also I. Adding the two gives 2I = the integral of 1 from 0 to π/2 = π/2. So I = π/4."}};
